.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3238:Rework the stop-before-start generation lifecycle while preserving the accepted startup-completion handoff fix, then run every required validation and attach evidence before the review handoff.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3241:  • Implement terminal generation consumption and retained-configuration cleanup
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3260:The board confirms this is the second focused rework. The accepted handoff-race fix is already present; the remaining defect is narrower: `stop()` currently treats the initial `.disconnected` state as a no-op, leaving the generation and configuration revivable. I’m reading the exact lifecycle code and test harness now so the fix stays inside the existing state model.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3276:The reported startup-completion health handoff race is closed. After the startup child returns, start now revalidates caller cancellation, environment cancellation, actor state, and termination before clearing startupTask with no intervening suspension. The two new deterministic tests force current-generation DNS health loss and caller cancellation into that handoff; neither issues a later stop, and both prove exactly-once reverse cleanup plus internal and external resource baselines.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3280:A stop that serializes before start is discarded, so the stopped generation can later start and the stop does not return retained configuration references to baseline.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3285:- stop returns immediately for disconnected without setting termination, consuming the generation, clearing the configuration reference, or starting cleanup (lines 389-395).
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3286:- hasStarted therefore remains false, and a later start passes its only consumption guard and transitions the same generation into startup (lines 358-369).
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3294:3. stop returns as a no-op; the configuration reference remains retained and the generation remains startable.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3297:This violates AC1 because the serialized stop event does not prevent later mutation/revival of the same generation, and AC5 because stop completes with a retained configuration reference instead of the recorded baseline. It also leaves the provider integration vulnerable to a stop/start scheduling inversion around the cross-actor runtime call.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3tlgwm/TASK-260715-3tlgwm_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:3301:- Make a stop that wins in the initial disconnected state terminally consume or cancel that generation, release its retained configuration reference, and prevent any later start from reviving it.
